    "created_at": "2019-10-10T12:41:29Z",
    "updated_at": "2019-10-10T12:41:29Z",
    "body": "I've got some ideas for things we could do when we get the ability to pull data in the background:\r\n\r\nI work on a project that uses several microservices as dependencies and the images I run locally in my dev environment get updated pretty frequently as the rest of the team works on the project. It's really easy to just keep working with outdated dependencies and not realize the updates are available.\r\n\r\nThe first idea is to use the daemon to parse the `docker-compose.yml` file and check for updated images and display text after the docker icon (`⇣`) if there are updates available.\r\n\r\nAnother potentially useful feature would be to display the uptime of the longest running container or configure a threshold for that uptime and display a warning icon (`!`) if a container has been running longer than the threshold time.\r\n\r\nI'm thinking something similar to the `git_status` module for how these icons might work:\r\n\r\n```\r\nkeawade/Projects/foo on  develop [✘!?] is 📦 v1.2.3 via ⬢ v12.11.1 with 🐋[⇣!]\r\n```",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "pin": null,
